(* Grammar of the language, in EBNF.
 *
 * Notation:  name ::= right side      a rule
 *            |                        alternatives
 *            [ x ]                    optional
 *            { x }                    zero or more
 *            "x"                      a terminal: a token the lexer emits, with this text
 *            name                     a non-terminal: it has a rule of its own
 *
 * Terminals come straight from the lexer (src/lexer.py):
 *   "i32" "i64" "bool"    keyword tokens: the type names
 *   "mut" "exit"          keyword tokens
 *   "if" "else" "while"   keyword tokens
 *   "struct" "fn"         keyword tokens
 *   "true" "false"        keyword tokens: the boolean literals
 *   "read" "print"        keyword tokens: the built-in functions, so never a name
 *   "{" "}"               block tokens
 *   ":=" "+" "-" "*"      operator tokens
 *   "==" "!="             operator tokens, two bytes each
 *   "!"                   operator token: a '!' not followed by '='
 *   "."                   operator token: a field of an object
 *   "->"                  operator token, two bytes: before a function's result type
 *   ","                   separator token: between values, parameters and arguments
 *   "(" ")"               separator tokens: only around parameters and arguments
 *   "[" "]"               separator tokens: only around a lambda's captures
 *   ident                 identifier token: letters, digits and _, not starting with a
 *                         digit, and not a keyword -- how it is spelled is the lexer's rule
 *   number                constant token: decimal digits, no sign
 *   string                string token: '"', any bytes but '"' and a newline, '"' --
 *                         no escape sequences, and closed on the line it opens on
 *
 *   NL                    the endline token that ends every line
 *
 * One line per declaration, assignment and exit. An if takes several: the condition
 * line, then "{", every line of the block and "}" each on a line of their own, and the
 * same again after an "else". A while takes the same shape, without the else. A struct
 * is its name line, "{", one line per field, its member functions and "}"; a function is
 * its signature line and a block, and so is a member function. NL is written out
 * wherever a line must end; blank lines carry nothing and are skipped. The lexer does
 * not pair the braces -- this grammar does.
 *
 * What this grammar deliberately cannot say (those are semantics, checked by the pass in
 * src/semantic.py): that a variable was declared before it is used, that it was declared
 * only once in its block, which declaration a name means when a block shadows it, that it
 * is "mut" before it is assigned, and every type rule -- that "+" takes two integers, that
 * a comparison gives a bool, that an i32 may widen into an i64, that the condition of an
 * if or a while and the operand of "!" are bools -- and every struct rule: that a type name
 * is a struct declared above, that a struct does not contain itself, that an object gets
 * one value per field, that a field chain names fields that exist, and that every link of
 * a chain written through is "mut" -- and every function rule: that a call names a
 * declared function with one argument of the right type per parameter, that exit in a
 * function has its result type, that a parameter is never assigned, and that a body sees
 * no top-level variable.
 *)

program   ::= { struct } { fn } { statement } exit

struct    ::= "struct" ident NL "{" NL field_decl { field_decl } { fn } "}" NL

field_decl::= type [ "mut" ] ident NL

fn        ::= "fn" ident ":=" "(" [ type ident { "," type ident } ] ")" "->" type NL block

statement ::= decl | assign | if | while | print | lambda

lambda    ::= "fn" ident ":=" "[" [ ident { "," ident } ] "]"
              "(" [ type ident { "," type ident } ] ")" "->" type NL block

decl      ::= type [ "mut" ] ident init

init      ::= "{" value { "," value } "}"

value     ::= expr | init

type      ::= "i32" | "i64" | "bool" | ident

assign    ::= ident { "." ident } ":=" expr

if        ::= "if" expr NL block [ "else" NL block ]

while     ::= "while" expr NL block

block     ::= "{" NL { statement } [ exit NL ] "}" NL

exit      ::= "exit" factor

expr      ::= arith [ ( "==" | "!=" ) arith ]

arith     ::= term { ( "+" | "-" ) term }

term      ::= factor { "*" factor }

factor    ::= number | "true" | "false" | ident | field | result | "!" factor

result    ::= ( call | member_call | read ) { "." ident { "." ident } "(" [ args ] ")" }
            [ "." ident { "." ident } ]

args      ::= expr { "," expr }

field     ::= ident "." ident { "." ident }

call      ::= ident "(" [ expr { "," expr } ] ")"

member_call ::= ident { "." ident } "." ident "(" [ expr { "," expr } ] ")"

read      ::= "read" "(" ")"

print     ::= "print" "(" ( string | expr ) ")"

(* Precedence and associativity come out of the shape of the last four rules, with no
 * table anywhere. A comparison binds weaker than arithmetic, so expr sits above arith and
 * both sides of "==" are whole chains: x * 2 != y + 5 compares the two results. Inside a
 * chain, arith calls term before it looks for a "+", so everything a "*" glues together is
 * already one node when the "+" is seen. Each loop builds the new node with what it has so
 * far as the left child, so 10 - 3 - 2 parses as (10 - 3) - 2 -- left associativity.
 *
 * expr has no loop around the comparison, so at most one comparison per expression.
 * Parentheses appear only in fn and call: there are still no grouping parentheses.
 *
 * "!" lives in factor, so it binds tighter than any operator and applies to the factor
 * right after it: !a == b compares !a with b. "!" factor recurses, so !!a is allowed.
 *
 * Note that exit takes a factor, not an expr: a single constant, boolean literal,
 * variable, field chain or call -- or one of those under "!" -- never an operation.
 *
 * The order of program is fixed: structs, then functions, then statements. A function's
 * block must end with exit, which the rule cannot say (a block's exit is optional), so the
 * parser checks it. fn and struct are not statements, so neither can stand in a block.
 *
 * A lambda is a statement: a function with a capture list in "[" "]", standing among the
 * top-level statements or in any block. It starts like fn, so the parser tells the two
 * apart by the fourth token -- "[" after fn ident ":=" -- and a fn without it inside a
 * block stays an error, as does a capture list on a member function. A lambda is the
 * first statement when it stands right after the functions. Its body ends with exit, as
 * a function's does. What a lambda may capture and see is the semantic pass's question.
 *
 * name(...) is a call whatever the name is: whether it is a function or an object whose
 * struct declares call -- triple(14), which is triple.call(14) -- depends on the names in
 * scope, which the grammar does not know.
 * A call is a factor, not a statement: it can stand only where its value is used.
 *
 * Inside a struct the fields come first and the member functions after them: a member
 * function is declared by the same rule fn as any other. In a chain, after every name
 * comes "." (the chain goes on), "(" (that name is a member function, called on the
 * chain before it) or anything else (the chain ends), so field, call and member_call
 * part on one token after a name, as before. Whether the chain before the last "." is an
 * object and not a type, and whether its struct declares that function, is the semantic
 * pass's question.
 *
 * A call does not end its chain: result goes on after its ")" into the value it returns
 * -- member functions called on it or on a field of it, p.moved(1).shift(2) and
 * c.grown(1).center.d2(), then maybe fields of the last result, c.grown(3).radius.
 * Whether a result has fields at all is the semantic pass's question too: a "." after a
 * call that returns an i32 is its error, at that ".". A result is a temporary copy, so a
 * result never stands to the left of ":=": assign starts with ident { "." ident }, and a
 * "(" there is an error.
 *
 * read and print are keywords, not names, so neither can name a variable, a function, a
 * parameter, a field or a member function. read is a factor with an i32 value; print is
 * a statement with no value, so it cannot stand where a value is expected. A string
 * stands only as the argument of print: no other rule names it.
 *
 * init recurses through value, so a {} may hold {}s: Ring g{{{10, 20}, r}, {{10, 20}, 2}}.
 * Whether a value may be a {} at all depends on the type of the field it lands in, which
 * the grammar does not know, so the semantic pass checks it: a {} only for a struct.
 *
 * type ::= ident is what makes a line starting with a name ambiguous: "Point p{...}" is a
 * decl, "p := ..." and "p.x := ..." are assigns. The second token decides -- a name or
 * "mut" means decl -- the one place the parser looks two tokens ahead.
 *
 * A block may not be empty: it holds at least one statement or an exit, which the rule
 * cannot say on its own, so the parser checks it. An exit ends its block, as it ends the
 * program: only the "}" may follow it. An if is a statement, so blocks nest.
 *)
